<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simeon I of Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">864/865–<a href="/years/927">927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Simeon the Great, Simeon I the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simeon I, known as Simeon the Great, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> from 893 to 927. Educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, he waged repeated wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, won a great victory at Achelous in 917 and claimed the title of tsar. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 924 but was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>. His reign was the golden age of Old Bulgarian literature, centred on his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Simeon was born in 864 or 865, the third son of Prince Boris I, who made Bulgaria Christian. Boris intended him for the church and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> at thirteen or fourteen, where he spent about a decade and became so fluent in Greek that Byzantine chronicles called him the half-Greek. About 888 he returned to the royal monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> and translated religious works from Greek into Medieval Bulgarian. When his elder brother Vladimir tried to reintroduce paganism, Boris had him imprisoned and blinded and appointed Simeon. An assembly at Preslav made Bulgarian the only language of state and church and moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> to Preslav.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The long peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> ended when Emperor Leo VI moved the market for Bulgarian goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, where merchants were heavily taxed. The Byzantines induced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 895, but in 896 Simeon, with Pecheneg allies, defeated them at the Battle of Southern Buh, and he then destroyed a Byzantine army at Bulgarophygon. Peace brought an annual tribute from Byzantium. After Leo's death in 912, Simeon reached Constantinople in 913 and was recognised as Emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> by Patriarch Nicholas Mystikos, though Zoe, mother of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, disowned the recognition in 914.</p><p>On 20 August 917, at the river Achelous, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> routed a Byzantine army, in one of the largest battles in medieval history. Simeon annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 924 after a revolt there. He met Romanos Lekapenos on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a> on 9 September 924, and about 925 raised the Bulgarian Church to a patriarchate; Pope John X recognised his imperial title. In 926 his troops were defeated by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomislav-of-croatia">Tomislav of Croatia</a> at the Battle of the Bosnian Highlands. Simeon died of heart failure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Preslav</a> on 27 May 927 and was succeeded by his son Peter I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Simeon's reign saw the first and most productive period of medieval Bulgarian literature. The Preslav Literary School and the Ohrid Literary School, where the Cyrillic alphabet was developed, produced translations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and of works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-of-caesarea">Basil of Caesarea</a>, and original works such as John Exarch's Six Days and Černorizec Hrabǎr's An Account of Letters. Disciples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, among them Naum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-of-preslav">Constantine of Preslav</a>, took part. Preslav had more than twenty cross-domed churches and a Golden Church, and it was Simeon's imperial capital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simeon I of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Simeon I</b> (893–927) fought Byzantium and its Magyar allies, calling in the Pechenegs, who drove the Magyars west, and won at Bulgarophygon in 896.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tsar <b>Simeon I of Bulgaria</b> employed them against the Magyars, and after the Battle of Southern Buh they drove the Magyars out of Etelköz and the Pontic steppes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He had defeated Hungarian raids and the army of <b>Simeon I of Bulgaria</b>, uniting the Croats in one kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Simeon I of Bulgaria</b> conquered Serbia in 924.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-serbia-early-medieval">Principality of Serbia (early medieval)</a></li><li><blockquote>Its best-known early ruler, Mihajlo Višević, governed in the early 10th century, allied with <b>Simeon I</b> of the First Bulgarian Empire and attending church councils at Split.</blockquote><a href="/state/zachlumia">Zachlumia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Veliki Preslav</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.36,27.13&amp;year=904" aria-label="Pliska on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5203 625 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Simeon I of Bulgaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5653 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5653 895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.36,27.13&amp;year=904">What was here in 904 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
